more than a dozen US cities have ordered  nighttime curfews as protests rage over  the death of a black man in police  custody curfews are enforced right now  in Los Angeles Philadelphia and Atlanta  among others the unrest has been  escalating across the country as  demonstrators took to the streets for  the fifth day running they're demanding  justice for George Floyd and an end to  police violence against minorities while  many of the protests have been peaceful  some demonstrators have sent police cars  on fire and there have been incidents of  looting in Minneapolis where the  protests began police have masked in  large numbers and deployed tear gas and  rubber bullets to clear demonstrators  breaking an 8 p.m. curfew let's talk now  to DW correspondent Stefon Simmons who  is on the streets of Minneapolis Stefon  so now that there is a curfew in place  what is the situation where you are  right now it is eerily quiet  I have to say ground zero for very  violent and not so violent protests who  in the last week is entirely quiet down  the curfew Wars and first with police  rather determined with the help of the  National Guard whether determined to let  not happen anymore what had going down  here the last few nights in the last few  days with buildings burning and  protesters or rioters in that case  looting and ransacking buildings and  stores and private houses even so we  could actually say that what happened  today is what happened to this car in  the back that any effort of any rioters  to set more buildings ablaze were  crashing into a determined police force  who took no prisoners in terms of using  all the tools they had at their disposal  beanbags teargas  and anything else they needed to  disperse the crowd here zone by zone  protected by the National Guard who set  up protective perimeters in which the  police then could move in and remove and  disburse protesters all right so it is  quiet now but we know that earlier this  evening you actually had your own  confrontation with the police let's have  a look at the video you sent us yes we  are we're doing a job and this is the  Senate Senate presentation so come on  where would you like us to go okay  Thank You officer all right Stefan well  that does appear to be a rather tense  situation but overall how would you  characterize police conduct in  Minneapolis well I would say they did  their job today at least as outlined by  their superior slash politicians  governor of the state as well as the  municipal government here remember they  didn't show up the last three days  before today or nights just yesterday we  had a curfew installed here too but  there was no police had no National  Guard actually even close to be able or  willing to enforce this and this has  changed today conduct as you can see in  this scene where my cameraman max Fergie  and I had this encounter just a little  bit of context three police cars had  actually encircled another TV production  team we don't know from what Network and  as we do in the field here we then rush  there and try to document this not to  interfere but document this and as you  saw nerves were a little bit frazzled  there I can somewhat understand this  because those those policemen are under  a lot of stress doing their job but of  course they should have let us work and  do our job but you know what did you  you're not I was or we were not about to  pick a fight tonight  all right Stefan Siemens reporting from  Minneapolis we appreciate it  well George Floyd's death has also  sparked demonstrations internationally  including here in the German capital  hundreds rallied in front of the US  Embassy in Berlin in solidarity several  groups had called for the rally on  social media under the motto justice for  George Floyd against racist police  violence  and just a few miles away graffiti of  Floyd was painted onto a section of the  Berlin wall together with the caption I  can't breathe for more let's talk to  Diana RC she's a Berlin based artist and  activist and also the founder of the  anti racism empowerment project white  guilt clean up Diana thank you so much  for joining us so what relevance does  the death of George Floyd have to people  outside the United States and especially  here in Germany hi good morning yeah I  think for black Germans particularly or  any black people any black tiles for  people who are living in Germany it's  just a sign of what is happening it's a  sign of what's to come in Germany if  Germany consider continues on the course  that they're currently on the United  Nations had put out a report what I  think two years ago and stated made some  very very clear statements about how  German laws do not do enough to protect  black people within the within the  borders of Germany all right well what  can you tell us about how people  currently are organizing in Berlin to  support people of color well I can't  speak too much to how people are  organizing to support people of color I  I work primarily in grassroots  organizing for supporting black people  and there's a lot of different things  going on there are several  collaborations and coalition's that have  been putting - being put together one of  the biggest issues that we have here in  Germany is that of aggregated statistic  racial statistics for police violence  aggregated racist statistics for  incarceration for dust in custody  none of this stuff is actually available  so several different groups have been  putting this to get together and working  on this like reach out and cop and there  are several big campaigns that have been  being supported for a very long time  that have started in the last years  including a banned racial profile  because now racial profiling is legal  and is legal in Berlin and each one  teach one has actually started this year  an afro census where they are asking  black diasporic people in Germany to  fill out a census so we can have a  better overview of what is really the  black experience in Germany well with  all of these demonstrations in the  United States across that country  outside the US do you see this enormous  response to George Floyd's death in  police custody somehow a kind of turning  point no not at all  I think one of the issues in Germany in  particular is that white anti-racist  activists feel that they need to come  and speak every time there is a deaths  or a murder in the United States but  there have been plenty of murders in  Germany plenty of deaths in custody that  have not been solved or a giallo  in 2008 2005 still has not been solved  he died in police custody and  mecklenburg-vorpommern the state in  which this occurred actually never the  state last year I believe it was last  year the year before last year the year  before they actually on the last day of  Black History Month decided not to  continue investigating this particular  case of this black man who died in  police custody in 2005 and and so for us  it's the thing of we see we see white  activist we see German activist come out  and speak about what is going on in the  United States in a very us centric way  without really acknowledging the  problems that are happening here in  Germany and how dangerous it is for germ  for black black diasporic people  including black Germans to exist inside  of Germany all right Diana  RC Berlin based artist and activist we  appreciate all your insights  thank you
 

